        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Environmental movement</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Where</span><span class="info-stat-val">worldwide: early centres in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>; later movements in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-korea">South Korea</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> and the rest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin America</a>, the Arab world and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">Romantic roots and smoke abatement in the 19th century; state forest conservation in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a> from 1842 and 1855; conservation and amenity societies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> from 1865 and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> from the late 19th century; the contemporary movement from the 1960s; international conferences in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stockholm">Stockholm</a> (1972) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-janeiro">Rio de Janeiro</a> (1992); environmental justice, indigenous rights and the climate crisis in recent decades</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Leading figures</span><span class="info-stat-val">Robert Hunter, Octavia Hill and Hardwicke Rawnsley, founders of the National Trust; John Ruskin; Sir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-blake">William Blake</a> Richmond of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">Coal</a> Smoke Abatement Society; the zoologist Alfred Newton; the foresters Alexander Gibson, Hugh Cleghorn and Dietrich Brandis; Henry David Thoreau, author of Walden; John Muir, founder of the Sierra Club; Rachel Carson, author of Silent Spring; Senator Gaylord Nelson; Chandi Prasad Bhatt in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Outcome</span><span class="info-stat-val">nature protection, clean air and clean water laws, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>’s Clean Air Act 1956 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>’ Clean Water, Clean Air and Endangered Species Acts of the 1970s; the National Trust, national parks and state forest departments; a movement still active and not always united, now much concerned with climate change</span></div>
