    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a> the movement grew from campaigns against the smoke of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/industrial-revolution">Industrial Revolution</a> and from the defence of countryside, forests and wildlife in the 19th century.</li><li>From the 1960s it campaigned against nuclear weapons and nuclear power, then acid rain, ozone depletion and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deforestation">deforestation</a>, and most recently climate change.</li><li>It is an international movement of many organisations and approaches, not always united in its goals, and has faced critics who see it as radical and misguided.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The first permanent, large-scale forest conservation programme in the world was introduced in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a> in 1855 under Governor-General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lord-dalhousie">Lord Dalhousie</a>, a model that soon spread to other colonies and to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>.</p></aside>
    </section>

